t protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>28. IS-IS Level 2 provides what?</h3><label><input name="q28" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> ARP suppression</label><label><input name="q28" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> VXLAN encapsulation</label><label><input name="q28" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> BGP authentication</label><label><input name="q28" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Inter-area/backbone routing</label><button onclick="cq(28)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f28"></div><span id="r28" style="display:none">Correct answer: Inter-area/backbone routing. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>29. MPLS primarily forwards on what?</h3><label><input name="q29" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Labels</label><label><input name="q29" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> VLAN IDs</label><label><input name="q29" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> OSPF areas</label><label><input name="q29" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> TCP ports</label><button onclick="cq(29)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f29"></div><span id="r29" style="display:none">Correct answer: Labels. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>30. MPLS L3VPN customer isolation uses what?</h3><label><input name="q30" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> STP instances</label><label><input name="q30" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> VRFs</label><label><input name="q30" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> BFD sessions</label><label><input name="q30" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Native VLANs</label><button onclick="cq(30)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f30"></div><span id="r30" style="display:none">Correct answer: VRFs. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>31. EVPN route type for MAC/IP endpoints?</h3><label><input name="q31" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Type 3</label><label><input name="q31" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Type 4</label><label><input name="q31" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Type 2</label><label><input name="q31" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Type 5</label><button onclick="cq(31)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f31"></div><span id="r31" style="display:none">Correct answer: Type 2. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>32. EVPN IP Prefix route type?</h3><label><input name="q32" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Type 2</label><label><input name="q32" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Type 3</label><label><input name="q32" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Type 1</label><label><input name="q32" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Type 5</label><button onclick="cq(32)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f32"></div><span id="r32" style="display:none">Correct answer: Type 5. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>33. EVPN IMET/BUM membership route type?</h3><label><input name="q33" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Type 3</label><label><input name="q33" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Type 5</label><label><input name="q33" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Type 2</label><label><input name="q33" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Type 4</label><button onclick="cq(33)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f33"></div><span id="r33" style="display:none">Correct answer: Type 3. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>34. VXLAN UDP destination port?</h3><label><input name="q34" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> 179</label><label><input name="q34" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> 4789</label><label><input name="q34" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> 89</label><label><input name="q34" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> 520</label><button onclick="cq(34)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f34"></div><span id="r34" style="display:none">Correct answer: 4789. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>35. Why plan underlay MTU for VXLAN?</h3><label><input name="q35" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> BGP updates need jumbo frames</label><label><input name="q35" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> OSPF hellos are 9KB</label><label><input name="q35" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Encapsulation adds outer-header overhead</label><label><input name="q35" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> LACP uses VXLAN</label><button onclick="cq(35)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f35"></div><span id="r35" style="display:none">Correct answer: Encapsulation adds outer-header overhead. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>36. Symmetric IRB means what?</h3><label><input name="q36" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> STP symmetry</label><label><input name="q36" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Route reflection</label><label><input name="q36" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> RIP summarization</label><label><input name="q36" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Distributed routing using tenant VRF/L3-VNI semantics at ingress and egress</label><button onclick="cq(36)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f36"></div><span id="r36" style="display:none">Correct answer: Distributed routing using tenant VRF/L3-VNI semantics at ingress and egress. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>37. Anycast gateway means what?</h3><label><input name="q37" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Same first-hop gateway IP/MAC on multiple VTEPs</label><label><input name="q37" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Route reflector</label><label><input name="q37" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> OSPF ASBR</label><label><input name="q37" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> DHCP server</label><button onclick="cq(37)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f37"></div><span id="r37" style="display:none">Correct answer: Same first-hop gateway IP/MAC on multiple VTEPs. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>38. BGP attribute normally preferred when higher?</h3><label><input name="q38" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> MED</label><label><input name="q38" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Local Preference</label><label><input name="q38" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> AS_PATH length</label><label><input name="q38" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Origin code</label><button onclick="cq(38)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f38"></div><span id="r38" style="display:none">Correct answer: Local Preference. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>39. BGP AS_PATH preference?</h3><label><input name="q39" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Longer is preferred</label><label><input name="q39" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> More communities wins</label><label><input name="q39" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Shorter is normally preferred</label><label><input name="q39" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Higher VNI wins</label><button onclick="cq(39)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f39"></div><span id="r39" style="display:none">Correct answer: Shorter is normally preferred. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>40. Why use route reflectors?</h3><label><input name="q40" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Replace IGP</label><label><input name="q40" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Encapsulate VXLAN</label><label><input name="q40" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Translate LSAs</label><label><input name="q40" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Reduce iBGP full-mesh requirements</label><button onclick="cq(40)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f40"></div><span id="r40" style="display:none">Correct answer: Reduce iBGP full-mesh requirements. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>41. Safest mutual-redistribution pattern?</h3><label><input name="q41" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Tag/classify origin and deny re-entry</label><label><input name="q41" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Redistribute all both ways</label><label><input name="q41" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Rely only on AD</label><label><input name="q41" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Set metrics zero</label><button onclick="cq(41)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f41"></div><span id="r41" style="display:none">Correct answer: Tag/classify origin and deny re-entry. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>42. Why is AD alone weak loop prevention?</h3><label><input name="q42" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> It is carried in AS_PATH</label><label><input name="q42" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> It selects local winners but does not preserve origin or prevent re-entry</label><label><input name="q42" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> It changes TTL</label><label><input name="q42" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> It applies only to static</label><button onclick="cq(42)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f42"></div><span id="r42" style="display:none">Correct answer: It selects local winners but does not preserve origin or prevent re-entry. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>43. Main risk of indiscriminate L2 stretch?</h3><label><input name="q43" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> BGP stops TCP</label><label><input name="q43" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> OSPF becomes classful</label><label><input name="q43" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Expanded BUM/loop/failure domains</label><label><input name="q43" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> MPLS labels vanish</label><button onclick="cq(43)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f43"></div><span id="r43" style="display:none">Correct answer: Expanded BUM/loop/failure domains. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>44. Cross-vendor LACP requires what?</h3><label><input name="q44" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Same hostname</label><label><input name="q44" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Same BGP ASN</label><label><input name="q44" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Different router IDs</label><label><input name="q44" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Compatible member/LACP/VLAN/MTU/bundle intent</label><button onclick="cq(44)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f44"></div><span id="r44" style="display:none">Correct answer: Compatible member/LACP/VLAN/MTU/bundle intent. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>45. LISP role in Cisco SD-Access?</h3><label><input name="q45" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Endpoint identity-to-location control-plane mapping</label><label><input name="q45" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> VXLAN encryption</label><label><input name="q45" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> STP elect